{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xurui","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xurui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Huai","name":"Huai, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JingJing","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, JingJing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Than Meets the Eye? Uncovering the Reasoning-Planning Disconnect in Training Vision-Language Driving Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-08T02:08:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:26:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Model (VLM) driving agents promise explainable end-to-end autonomy by first producing natural-language reasoning and then predicting trajectory planning. However, whether planning is causally driven by this reasoning remains a critical but unverified assumption. To investigate this, we build DriveMind, a large-scale driving Visual Question Answering corpus with plan-aligned Chain-of-Thought (CoT), automatically generated from nuPlan. Our data generation process converts sensors and annotations into structured inputs and, crucially, separates priors from to-be-reasoned signals, enabling clean information ablations. Using DriveMind, we train representative VLM agents with Supervised Fine-Tuning and Group Relative Policy Optimization and evaluate them with nuPlan's metrics. Our results, unfortunately, indicate a consistent causal disconnect in reasoning-planning: removing ego/navigation priors causes large drops in planning scores, whereas removing CoT produces only minor changes. Attention analysis further shows that planning primarily focuses on priors rather than the CoT. Based on this evidence, we propose the Reasoning-Planning Decoupling Hypothesis, positing that the training-yielded reasoning is an ancillary byproduct rather than a causal mediator. To enable efficient diagnosis, we introduce a novel, training-free probe that measures an agent's reliance on priors by evaluating its planning robustness against minor input perturbations. In summary, we provide the community with a new dataset and a diagnostic tool to evaluate the causal fidelity of future models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"May","name":"May, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diganta","familyName":"Misra","name":"Misra, Diganta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjali","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Anjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justine","familyName":"Gehring","name":"Gehring, Justine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio Soares Ribeiro","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, Silvio Soares Ribeiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FreshBrew: A Benchmark for Evaluating AI Agents on Java Code Migration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:39:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-03T01:00:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:25:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3744916.3773214","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI coding assistants are rapidly becoming integral to modern software development. A key challenge in this space is the continual need to migrate and modernize codebases in response to evolving software ecosystems. Traditionally, such migrations have relied on rule-based systems and human intervention. With the advent of powerful large language models (LLMs), AI-driven agentic frameworks offer a promising alternative-but their effectiveness has not been systematically evaluated. In this paper, we introduce \\FreshBrew{}\\footnote{\\url{https://github.com/mrcabbage972/freshbrew}}, a novel benchmark for evaluating AI agents on project-level Java migrations, with a specific focus on measuring an agent's ability to preserve program semantics and avoid reward hacking, which we argue requires projects with high test coverage for a rigorous and reliable evaluation. We benchmark several state-of-the-art LLMs, and compare their performance against established rule-based tools. Our evaluation on a challenging subset of 228 public Maven repositories that build on JDK 8, fail on JDK 17, and have high test coverage shows that the top-performing model, Gemini 2.5 Flash, can successfully migrate 52.3\\% of projects to JDK 17. Our empirical analysis reveals novel insights into the critical strengths and limitations of current agentic approaches, offering actionable insights into their real-world applicability. Our empirical study reveals failure modes of current AI agents in realistic Java modernization tasks, providing a foundation for evaluating trustworthy code-migration systems. By releasing \\FreshBrew{}, we aim to facilitate rigorous, reproducible evaluation and catalyze progress in AI-driven codebase modernization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seunghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taesun","familyName":"Yeom","name":"Yeom, Taesun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonpyo","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Wonpyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyuyeun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kyuyeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaeho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation Quantization of Vision Encoders Needs Prefixing Registers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:27:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:33:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T12:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:26:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T12:18:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:55:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-08T06:28:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-09T00:24:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large pretrained vision encoders are central to multimodal intelligence, powering applications from on-device vision processing to vision-language models. Since these applications often demand real-time processing of massive visual data, reducing the inference cost of vision encoders is critical. Quantization offers a practical path, but it remains challenging even at 8-bit precision due to so-called outliers. In this work, we propose $\\textit{RegCache}$, a training-free algorithm that mitigates outliers in large-scale pretrained vision encoders and serves as a plug-in module that can be applied on top of other quantization methods. RegCache introduces outlier-prone yet semantically meaningless prefix tokens to the vision encoder, which prevent other tokens from having outliers. Notably, we observe that outliers in vision encoders behave differently from those in language models, motivating two technical innovations: middle-layer prefixing and token deletion. Experimental results show that our method consistently improves quantized model performance across various vision encoders, particularly in extremely low-bit regimes (e.g., 4-bit)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ECCV 2026. Code: https://github.com/spbob0418/RegCache"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-09T02:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruohan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ruohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoxiong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Guoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xintao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aria: An Agent For Retrieval and Iterative Auto-Formalization via Dependency Graph"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-02T09:05:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate auto-formalization of theorem statements is essential for advancing automated discovery and verification of research-level mathematics, yet remains a major bottleneck for LLMs due to hallucinations, semantic mismatches, and their inability to synthesize new definitions. To tackle these issues, we present Aria (Agent for Retrieval and Iterative Autoformalization), a system for conjecture-level formalization in Lean that emulates human expert reasoning via a two-phase Graph-of-Thought process: recursively decomposing statements into a dependency graph and then constructing formalizations from grounded concepts. To ensure semantic correctness, we introduce AriaScorer, a checker that retrieves definitions from Mathlib for term-level grounding, enabling rigorous and reliable verification. We evaluate Aria on diverse benchmarks. On ProofNet, it achieves 91.6% compilation success rate and 68.5% final accuracy, surpassing previous methods. On FATE-X, a suite of challenging algebra problems from research literature, it outperforms the best baseline with 44.0% vs. 24.0% final accuracy. On a dataset of homological conjectures, Aria reaches 42.9% final accuracy while all other models score 0%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:13:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04773","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04773","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04773","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jianxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyuan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Haoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongzhe","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiantian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tiantian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houde","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Houde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:49:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T11:41:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities learned from vast corpora, concerns regarding data privacy and safety are receiving increasing attention. LLM unlearning, which aims to remove the influence of specific data while preserving overall model utility, is becoming an important research area. One of the mainstream unlearning classes is optimization-based methods, which achieve forgetting directly through fine-tuning, exemplified by Negative Preference Optimization (NPO). However, NPO's effectiveness is limited by its inherent lack of explicit positive preference signals. Attempts to introduce such signals by constructing preferred responses often necessitate domain-specific knowledge or well-designed prompts, fundamentally restricting their generalizability. In this paper, we shift the focus to the distribution-level, directly targeting the next-token probability distribution instead of entire responses, and derive a novel unlearning algorithm termed \\textbf{Di}stribution \\textbf{P}reference \\textbf{O}ptimization (DiPO). We show that the requisite preference distribution pairs for DiPO, which are distributions over the model's output tokens, can be constructed by selectively amplifying or suppressing the model's high-confidence output logits, thereby effectively overcoming NPO's limitations. We theoretically prove the consistency of DiPO's loss function with the desired unlearning direction. Extensive experiments demonstrate that DiPO achieves a strong trade-off between model utility and forget quality. Notably, DiPO attains the highest forget quality on the TOFU benchmark, and maintains leading scalability and sustainability in utility preservation on the MUSE benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04773","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04584","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04584","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04584","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernando","familyName":"López","name":"López, Fernando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santosh","familyName":"Kesiraju","name":"Kesiraju, Santosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Luque","name":"Luque, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustness assessment of large audio language models in multiple-choice evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-24T15:08:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-25T01:01:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large audio language models (LALMs) have primarily been assessed using a multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) framework. However, subtle changes, such as shifting the order of choices, result in substantially different results. Existing MCQA frameworks do not account for this variability and report a single accuracy number per benchmark or category. We dive into the MCQA evaluation framework and conduct a systematic study spanning three benchmarks (MMAU, MMAR and MMSU) and four models: Audio Flamingo 2, Audio Flamingo 3, Qwen2.5-Omni-7B-Instruct, and Kimi-Audio-7B-Instruct. Our findings indicate that models are sensitive not only to the ordering of choices, but also to the paraphrasing of the question and the choices. Finally, we propose a simpler evaluation protocol and metric that account for subtle variations and provide a more detailed evaluation report of LALMs within the MCQA framework."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04584","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-25T02:18:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04767","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04767","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonjun","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wonjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Galim","name":"Galim, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghyuk","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Seunghyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Minjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuibai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuibai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Coleman","familyName":"Hooper","name":"Hooper, Coleman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuezhou","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyung Il","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Hyung Il","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nam Ik","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nam Ik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangwook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kangwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParallelBench: Understanding the Trade-offs of Parallel Decoding in Diffusion LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:41:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T05:37:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:28:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While most autoregressive LLMs are constrained to one-by-one decoding, diffusion LLMs (dLLMs) have attracted growing interest for their potential to dramatically accelerate inference through parallel decoding. Despite this promise, the conditional independence assumption in dLLMs causes parallel decoding to ignore token dependencies, inevitably degrading generation quality when these dependencies are strong. However, existing works largely overlook these inherent challenges, and evaluations on standard benchmarks (e.g., math and coding) are not sufficient to capture the quality degradation caused by parallel decoding. To address this gap, we first provide an information-theoretic analysis of parallel decoding. We then conduct case studies on analytically tractable synthetic list operations from both data distribution and decoding strategy perspectives, offering quantitative insights that highlight the fundamental limitations of parallel decoding. Building on these insights, we propose ParallelBench, the first benchmark specifically designed for dLLMs, featuring realistic tasks that are trivial for humans and autoregressive LLMs yet exceptionally challenging for dLLMs under parallel decoding. Using ParallelBench, we systematically analyze both dLLMs and autoregressive LLMs, revealing that: (i) dLLMs under parallel decoding can suffer dramatic quality degradation in real-world scenarios, and (ii) current parallel decoding strategies struggle to adapt their degree of parallelism based on task difficulty, thus failing to achieve meaningful speedup without compromising quality. Our findings underscore the pressing need for innovative decoding methods that can overcome the current speed-quality trade-off. We release our benchmark to help accelerate the development of truly efficient dLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026. Project Page: https://parallelbench.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04767","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Riedl","name":"Riedl, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent Coordination in Multi-Agent Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T21:22:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T14:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T21:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When are multi-agent LLM systems merely a collection of individual agents versus an integrated collective with higher-order structure? We introduce an information-theoretic framework to test -- in a purely data-driven way -- whether multi-agent systems show signs of higher-order structure. This information decomposition lets us measure whether dynamical emergence is present in multi-agent LLM systems, localize it, and distinguish spurious temporal coupling from performance-relevant cross-agent synergy. We implement a practical criterion and an emergence capacity criterion operationalized as partial information decomposition of time-delayed mutual information (TDMI). We apply our framework to experiments using a simple guessing game without direct agent communication and minimal group-level feedback with three randomized interventions. Groups in the control condition exhibit strong temporal synergy but little coordinated alignment across agents. Assigning a persona to each agent introduces stable identity-linked differentiation. Combining personas with an instruction to ``think about what other agents might do'' shows identity-linked differentiation and goal-directed complementarity across agents. Taken together, our framework establishes that multi-agent LLM systems can be steered with prompt design from mere aggregates to higher-order collectives. Our results are robust across emergence measures and entropy estimators, and not explained by coordination-free baselines or temporal dynamics alone. Without attributing human-like cognition to the agents, the patterns of interaction we observe mirror well-established principles of collective intelligence in human groups: effective performance requires both alignment on shared objectives and complementary contributions across members."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachneet","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Rachneet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishan","familyName":"Srishankar","name":"Srishankar, Nishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumitra","familyName":"Ganesh","name":"Ganesh, Sumitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuela","familyName":"Veloso","name":"Veloso, Manuela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ChartAgent: A Multimodal Agent for Visually Grounded Reasoning in Complex Chart Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:05:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T06:02:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T20:02:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-10T00:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent multimodal LLMs have shown promise in chart-based visual question answering, but their performance declines sharply on unannotated charts-those requiring precise visual interpretation rather than relying on textual shortcuts. To address this, we introduce ChartAgent, a novel agentic framework that explicitly performs visual reasoning directly within the chart's spatial domain. Unlike textual chain-of-thought reasoning, ChartAgent iteratively decomposes queries into visual subtasks and actively manipulates and interacts with chart images through specialized actions such as drawing annotations, cropping regions (e.g., segmenting pie slices, isolating bars), and localizing axes, using a library of chart-specific vision tools to fulfill each subtask. This iterative reasoning process closely mirrors human cognitive strategies for chart comprehension. ChartAgent achieves state-of-the-art accuracy on the ChartBench and ChartX benchmarks, surpassing prior methods by up to 16.07% absolute gain overall and 17.31% on unannotated, numerically intensive queries. Furthermore, our analyses show that ChartAgent is (a) effective across diverse chart types, (b) achieves the highest scores across varying visual and reasoning complexity levels, and (c) serves as a plug-and-play framework that boosts performance across diverse underlying LLMs. Our work is among the first to demonstrate visually grounded reasoning for chart understanding using tool-augmented multimodal agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main Conference). Also presented as an oral paper at the NeurIPS 2025 Multimodal Algorithmic Reasoning Workshop (https://marworkshop.github.io/neurips25/)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-10T02:30:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeong Kyu","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hyeong Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Distillation Data from Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T06:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning distillation has emerged as a prevailing paradigm for transferring reasoning capabilities from large reasoning models to small language models. Yet, reasoning distillation risks data contamination: benchmark data may inadvertently be included in the distillation data, thereby inflating model performance metrics. In this work, we formally define the distillation data detection task, which determines whether a given question is included in the model's distillation data. The unique challenge of this task lies in the partial availability of distillation data. To address this, we propose Token Probability Deviation (TPD), a detection method that leverages the probability patterns of output tokens generated by the model instead of input tokens. Our method is motivated by the observation that seen questions tend to elicit more near-deterministic tokens generated by the models than unseen ones. Our TPD score is thus designed to quantify the token-level deviation of generated tokens from a high-confidence reference probability. Consequently, seen questions can yield substantially lower TPD scores than unseen ones, enabling strong detection performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, improving detection AUC by up to 31% on distillation datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From surveillance to signalling: escalation channels as environmental controls for agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:37:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T21:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When AI agents operating with access to sensitive information encounter a conflict between completing an assigned task and following rules or ethical constraints, they can resort to unsanctioned behaviour. Existing inference time safety work addresses this primarily through monitoring and access restriction. We investigate a complementary and under-explored layer: environmental controls that act on the agent's decision context at the point of conflict, making it more likely that the agent takes an authorised alternative path rather than an unsanctioned one. Drawing on Situational Crime Prevention (SCP), a framework used in human insider risk management to make harmful actions less rewarding and compliant actions more viable by design choices in the environment, we design and evaluate escalation channels as a concrete instantiation of this control class. An escalation channel provides an agent with a formal, out-of-band route to surface a conflict to an independent authority. We evaluate two designs: a simple email escalation and an instrumentally credible channel that guarantees a 30-minute pause and independent review, making the authorised path genuinely useful for goal achievement rather than merely nominally available. Across 10 frontier LLMs using the agentic task-rule conflict scenario of Lynch et al. (2025), we find that without any control the harmful action rate is 38.73%. A simple escalation channel reduces this to 5.92%; the instrumentally credible channel reduces it further to 1.21%, a statistically significant improvement observed in all 10 models tested across 24,000 samples. Our results suggest that the instrumental credibility of the authorised alternative matters considerably, and that environmental control design is a productive and largely unexplored addition to the defence-in-depth toolkit for agentic AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikki Lijing","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Nikki Lijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicklas","familyName":"Majamaki","name":"Majamaki, Nicklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navdeep","familyName":"Jaitly","name":"Jaitly, Navdeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-An","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T17:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:01:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:35:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-11T22:01:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-15T01:09:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T09:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:38:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-23T01:00:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate their reasoning ability through chain-of-thought (CoT) generation. However, LLM's autoregressive decoding may limit the ability to revisit and refine earlier tokens in a holistic manner, which can also lead to inefficient exploration for diverse solutions. In this paper, we propose LaDiR} (Latent Diffusion Reasoner), a novel reasoning framework that unifies the expressiveness of continuous latent representation with the iterative refinement capabilities of latent diffusion models for an existing LLM. We first construct a structured latent reasoning space using a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) that encodes text reasoning steps into blocks of thought tokens, preserving semantic information and interpretability while offering compact but expressive representations. Subsequently, we utilize a latent diffusion model that learns to denoise a block of latent thought tokens with a blockwise bidirectional attention mask, enabling longer horizon and iterative refinement with adaptive test-time compute. This design, combined with explicit diversity guidance during diffusion inference, enables the generation of multiple diverse reasoning trajectories that explore distinct regions of the latent space, rather than producing repetitive solutions as often occurs in standard autoregressive sampling. We conduct evaluations on a suite of mathematical reasoning, code generation and puzzle planning benchmarks. Empirical results show that LaDiR consistently improves accuracy, diversity, and interpretability over existing autoregressive, diffusion-based, and latent reasoning methods, revealing a new paradigm for text reasoning with latent diffusion."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenda","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wenda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqing","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Yanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junle","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisheng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Yisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei-Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fei-Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Plug-and-Play Dramaturge: A Divide-and-Conquer Approach for Iterative Narrative Script Refinement via Collaborative LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:20:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T08:29:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T04:52:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLMs have been widely adopted for creative content generation, a single-pass process often struggles to produce high-quality long narratives. How to effectively revise and improve long narrative scripts like scriptwriters remains a significant challenge, as it demands a comprehensive understanding of the entire context to identify global structural issues and local detailed flaws, as well as coordinating revisions at multiple granularities and locations. Direct modifications by LLMs typically introduce inconsistencies between local edits and the overall narrative requirements. To address these issues, we propose Dramaturge, a task and feature oriented divide-and-conquer approach powered by hierarchical multiple LLM agents. It consists of a Global Review stage to grasp the overall storyline and structural issues, a Scene-level Review stage to pinpoint detailed scene and sentence flaws, and a Hierarchical Coordinated Revision stage that coordinates and integrates structural and detailed improvements throughout the script. The top-down task flow ensures that high-level strategies guide local modifications, maintaining contextual consistency. The review and revision workflow follows a coarse-to-fine iterative process, continuing through multiple rounds until no further substantive improvements can be made. Comprehensive experiments show that Dramaturge significantly outperforms all baselines in terms of script-level overall quality and scene-level details. Our approach is plug-and-play and can be easily integrated into existing methods to improve the generated scripts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangmin","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Sangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilge","familyName":"Acun","name":"Acun, Bilge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Yu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chien-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haroun","familyName":"Habeeb","name":"Habeeb, Haroun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seungyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carole-Jean","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Carole-Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Architectures for Language Models: Systematic Analysis and Design Insights"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:30:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T04:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:29:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T13:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:56:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent progress in large language models demonstrates that hybrid architectures--combining self-attention mechanisms with structured state space models like Mamba--can achieve a compelling balance between modeling quality and computational efficiency, particularly for long-context tasks. While these hybrid models show promising performance, systematic comparisons of hybridization strategies and analyses on the key factors behind their effectiveness have not been clearly shared to the community. In this work, we present a holistic evaluation of hybrid architectures based on inter-layer (sequential) or intra-layer (parallel) fusion. We comprehensively evaluate these designs across multiple dimensions: language modeling and downstream task performance, long-context capabilities, scaling analysis, and training and inference efficiency. By investigating the core characteristics of their computational primitive, we identify the most critical elements for each hybridization strategy and further propose optimal design recipes for hybrid models. Our comprehensive analysis provides practical guidance and valuable insights for developing hybrid language models, facilitating the optimization of architectural configurations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 8 figures, 22 tables;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:53:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:53:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feihao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating LLMs for Demographic-Targeted Social Bias Detection: A Comprehensive Benchmark Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:45:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:23:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T15:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T02:02:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-09T11:53:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:48:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale web-scraped text corpora used to train general-purpose AI models often contain harmful demographic-targeted social biases, creating a regulatory need for data auditing and developing scalable bias-detection methods. Although prior work has investigated biases in text datasets and related detection methods, these studies remain narrow in scope. They typically focus on a single content type (e.g., hate speech), cover limited demographic axes, overlook biases affecting multiple demographics simultaneously, and analyze limited techniques. Consequently, practitioners lack a holistic understanding of the strengths and limitations of recent large language models (LLMs) for automated bias detection. In this study, we conduct a comprehensive benchmark study on English texts to assess the ability of LLMs in detecting demographic-targeted social biases. To align with regulatory requirements, we frame bias detection as a multi-label task of detecting targeted identities using a demographic-focused taxonomy. We then systematically evaluate models across scales and techniques, including prompting, in-context learning, and fine-tuning. Using twelve datasets spanning diverse content types and demographics, our study demonstrates the promise of fine-tuned smaller models for scalable detection. However, our analyses also expose persistent gaps across demographic axes and multi-demographic targeted biases, underscoring the need for more effective and scalable detection frameworks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Uzan","name":"Uzan, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asaf","familyName":"Yehudai","name":"Yehudai, Asaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roi","familyName":"pony","name":"pony, Roi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eyal","familyName":"Shnarch","name":"Shnarch, Eyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel","familyName":"Gera","name":"Gera, Ariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided Query Refinement: Multimodal Hybrid Retrieval with Test-Time Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:12:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T19:30:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T22:22:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:11:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal encoders have pushed the boundaries of visual document retrieval, matching textual query tokens directly to image patches and achieving state-of-the-art performance on public benchmarks. Recent models relying on this paradigm have massively scaled the sizes of their query and document representations, presenting obstacles to deployment and scalability in real-world pipelines. Furthermore, purely vision-centric approaches may be constrained by the inherent modality gap still exhibited by modern vision-language models. In this work, we connect these challenges to the paradigm of hybrid retrieval, investigating whether a lightweight dense text retriever can enhance a stronger vision-centric model. Existing hybrid methods, which rely on coarse-grained fusion of ranks or scores, fail to exploit the rich interactions within each model's representation space. To address this, we introduce Guided Query Refinement (GQR), a novel test-time optimization method that refines a primary retriever's query embedding using guidance from a complementary retriever's scores. Through extensive experiments on visual document retrieval benchmarks, we demonstrate that GQR allows vision-centric models to match the performance of models with significantly larger representations, while being up to 14x faster and requiring 54x less memory. Our findings show that GQR effectively pushes the Pareto frontier for performance and efficiency in multimodal retrieval. We release our code at https://github.com/IBM/test-time-hybrid-retrieval"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Courant","name":"Courant, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Loiseaux","name":"Loiseaux, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Christie","name":"Christie, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vicky","familyName":"Kalogeiton","name":"Kalogeiton, Vicky","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pulp Motion: Framing-aware multimodal camera and human motion generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:58:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T15:04:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T01:00:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Treating human motion and camera trajectory generation separately overlooks a core principle of cinematography: the tight interplay between actor performance and camera work in the screen space. In this paper, we are the first to cast this task as a text-conditioned joint generation, aiming to maintain consistent on-screen framing while producing two heterogeneous, yet intrinsically linked, modalities: human motion and camera trajectories. We propose a simple, model-agnostic framework that enforces multimodal coherence via an auxiliary modality: the on-screen framing induced by projecting human joints onto the camera. This on-screen framing provides a natural and effective bridge between modalities, promoting consistency and leading to more precise joint distribution. We first design a joint autoencoder that learns a shared latent space, together with a lightweight linear transform from the human and camera latents to a framing latent. We then introduce auxiliary sampling, which exploits this linear transform to steer generation toward a coherent framing modality. To support this task, we also introduce the PulpMotion dataset, a human-motion and camera-trajectory dataset with rich captions, and high-quality human motions. Extensive experiments across DiT- and MAR-based architectures show the generality and effectiveness of our method in generating on-frame coherent human-camera motions, while also achieving gains on textual alignment for both modalities. Our qualitative results yield more cinematographically meaningful framings setting the new state of the art for this task. Code, models and data are available in our \\href{https://www.lix.polytechnique.fr/vista/projects/2025_pulpmotion_courant/}{project page}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://www.lix.polytechnique.fr/vista/projects/2025_pulpmotion_courant/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04618","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04618","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changran","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Changran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubhangi","familyName":"Upasani","name":"Upasani, Shubhangi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fenglu","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Fenglu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamsidhar","familyName":"Kamanuru","name":"Kamanuru, Vamsidhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jay","familyName":"Rainton","name":"Rainton, Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengmeng","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Mengmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Urmish","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Urmish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunle","familyName":"Olukotun","name":"Olukotun, Kunle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Context Engineering: Evolving Contexts for Self-Improving Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:22:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-29T09:18:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:55:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) applications such as agents and domain-specific reasoning increasingly rely on context adaptation: modifying inputs with instructions, strategies, or evidence, rather than weight updates. Prior approaches improve usability but often suffer from brevity bias, which drops domain insights for concise summaries, and from context collapse, where iterative rewriting erodes details over time. We introduce ACE (Agentic Context Engineering), a framework that treats contexts as evolving playbooks that accumulate, refine, and organize strategies through a modular process of generation, reflection, and curation. ACE prevents collapse with structured, incremental updates that preserve detailed knowledge and scale with long-context models. Across agent and domain-specific benchmarks, ACE optimizes contexts both offline (e.g., system prompts) and online (e.g., agent memory), consistently outperforming strong baselines: +10.6% on agents and +8.6% on finance, while significantly reducing adaptation latency and rollout cost. Notably, ACE could adapt effectively without labeled supervision and instead by leveraging natural execution feedback. On the AppWorld leaderboard, ACE matches the top-ranked production-level agent on the overall average and surpasses it on the harder test-challenge split, despite using a smaller open-source model. These results show that comprehensive, evolving contexts enable scalable, efficient, and self-improving LLM systems with low overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026; 32 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04618","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":3,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04607","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04607","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Imperative to Declarative: Towards LLM-friendly OS Interfaces for Boosted Computer-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:14:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T11:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:47:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767295.3803576","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computer-use agents (CUAs) powered by large language models (LLMs) have emerged as a promising approach to automating computer tasks, yet they struggle with the existing human-oriented OS interfaces - graphical user interfaces (GUIs). GUIs force LLMs to decompose high-level goals into lengthy, error-prone sequences of fine-grained actions, resulting in low success rates and an excessive number of LLM calls.\n We propose Declarative Model Interface (DMI), an abstraction that transforms existing GUIs into three declarative primitives: access, state, and observation, thereby providing novel OS interfaces tailored for LLM agents. Our key idea is policy-mechanism separation: LLMs focus on high-level semantic planning (policy) while DMI handles low-level navigation and interaction (mechanism). DMI does not require modifying the application source code or relying on application programming interfaces (APIs).\n We evaluate DMI with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, PowerPoint, Excel) on Windows. Integrating DMI into a leading GUI-based agent baseline improves task success rates by 67% and reduces interaction steps by 43.5%. Notably, DMI completes over 61% of successful tasks with a single LLM call."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04607","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dachuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abedelkadir","familyName":"Asi","name":"Asi, Abedelkadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Keying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiangchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Leyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SwiReasoning: Switch-Thinking in Latent and Explicit for Pareto-Superior Reasoning LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T16:34:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:33:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-01T18:32:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:21:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work shows that, beyond discrete reasoning through explicit chain-of-thought steps, which are limited by the boundaries of natural languages, large language models (LLMs) can also reason continuously in latent space, allowing richer information per step and thereby improving token efficiency. Despite this promise, latent reasoning still faces two challenges, especially in training-free settings: 1) purely latent reasoning broadens the search distribution by maintaining multiple implicit paths, which diffuses probability mass, introduces noise, and impedes convergence to a single high-confidence solution, thereby hurting accuracy; and 2) overthinking persists even without explicit text, wasting tokens and degrading efficiency. To address these issues, we introduce SwiReasoning, a training-free framework for LLM reasoning which features two key innovations: 1) SwiReasoning dynamically switches between explicit and latent reasoning, guided by block-wise confidence estimated from entropy trends in next-token distributions, to balance exploration and exploitation and promote timely convergence. 2) By limiting the maximum number of thinking-block switches, SwiReasoning curbs overthinking and improves token efficiency across varying problem difficulties. On widely used mathematics, STEM, coding, and general benchmarks, SwiReasoning consistently improves average accuracy by 1.8%-3.1% across reasoning LLMs of different model families and scales. Furthermore, under constrained budgets, SwiReasoning improves average token efficiency by 57%-79%, with larger gains as budgets tighten."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026. Code: https://github.com/sdc17/SwiReasoning, Website: https://swireasoning.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Janapa","familyName":"Reddi","name":"Reddi, Vijay Janapa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slm-mux: Orchestrating small language models for reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:49:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T06:48:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid development of language models, the number of small language models (SLMs) has grown significantly. Although they do not achieve state-of-the-art accuracy, they are more efficient and often excel at specific tasks. This raises a natural question: can multiple SLMs be orchestrated into a system where each contributes effectively, achieving higher accuracy than any individual model? Existing orchestration methods have primarily targeted frontier models (e.g., GPT-4) and perform suboptimally when applied to SLMs. To address this gap, we propose a three-stage approach for orchestrating SLMs. First, we introduce SLM-MUX, a multi-model architecture that effectively coordinates multiple SLMs. Building on this, we develop two optimization strategies: (i) a model selection search that identifies the most complementary SLMs from a given pool, and (ii) test-time scaling tailored to SLM-MUX. Our approach delivers strong results: Compared to existing orchestration methods, our approach achieves up to 13.4% improvement on MATH, 8.8% on GPQA, and 7.0% on GSM8K. With just two SLMs, SLM-MUX outperforms Qwen 2.5 72B on GPQA and GSM8K, and matches its performance on MATH. We further provide theoretical analyses to substantiate the advantages of our method. Additional experiments show that the core principle of SLM-MUX extends to open-ended generation tasks (e.g., HumanEval) and benefits other model classes, including frontier LLMs and domain-specific fine-tuned SLMs. In summary, we demonstrate that SLMs can be effectively orchestrated into more accurate and efficient systems through the proposed approach. The project page is available at https://slm-mux.github.io/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaofeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cihang","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Cihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaxiu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Huaxiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Alignment Tipping Process: How Self-Evolution Pushes LLM Agents Off the Rails"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T20:59:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:08:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Model (LLM) agents increasingly gain self-evolutionary capabilities to adapt and refine their strategies through real-world interaction, their long-term reliability becomes a critical concern. We identify the Alignment Tipping Process (ATP), a critical post-deployment risk unique to self-evolving LLM agents. Unlike training-time failures, ATP arises when continual interaction drives agents to abandon alignment constraints established during training in favor of reinforced, self-interested strategies. We formalize and analyze ATP through two complementary paradigms: Self-Interested Exploration, where repeated high-reward deviations induce individual behavioral drift, and Imitative Strategy Diffusion, where deviant behaviors spread across multi-agent systems. Building on these paradigms, we construct controllable testbeds and benchmark both open and closed-source LLMs. Our experiments show that alignment benefits erode rapidly under self-evolution, with initially aligned models converging toward unaligned states. In multi-agent settings, successful violations diffuse quickly, leading to collective misalignment. Moreover, current reinforcement learning-based alignment methods provide limited defenses against alignment tipping. These findings demonstrate that alignment of LLM agents is not a static property but a fragile and dynamic one, vulnerable to feedback-driven decay during deployment. Our data and code are available at https://github.com/aiming-lab/ATP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoxiong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shaoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiwen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Peiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyi","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PatternKV: Flattening KV Representation Expands Quantization Headroom"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:09:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T06:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:30:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV cache in autoregressive LLMs eliminates redundant recomputation but has emerged as the dominant memory and bandwidth bottleneck during inference, notably with long contexts and test-time scaling. KV quantization is a key lever for reducing cache cost, but accuracy drops sharply as the native KV distribution lacks flatness and thus maintains a wide quantization range. Prior work focuses on isolating outliers, which caps their error but fails to flatten the overall distribution, leaving performance fragile under low-bit settings. In this work, we show that the K cache maintains a stable, context-evolving structure, while the V cache carries latent semantic regularities, with both contributing to the organization of vectors into shared patterns. Building on these insights, we propose PatternKV, a pattern-aligned residual quantization scheme. It mines representative pattern vectors online, aligns each KV vector to its nearest pattern, and quantizes only the residual. This reshaping of the KV distribution flattens the quantization target and narrows its range, thereby improving the fidelity of low-bit KV quantization. Across long-context and test-time scaling settings on multiple backbones, PatternKV delivers consistent 2-bit gains, with a 0.08% average 4-bit drop relative to FP16, improves test-time scaling accuracy by 10% on average, and raises throughput by 1.5x while supporting 1.25x larger batches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanming","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xuanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianpeng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Jianpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-you","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hong-you","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satya Narayan","familyName":"Shukla","name":"Shukla, Satya Narayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhijeet","familyName":"Awasthi","name":"Awasthi, Abhijeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xichen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaitanya","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Chaitanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shlok Kumar","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Shlok Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghuan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yonghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ser-Nam","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Ser-Nam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aashu","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Aashu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjun","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Xiangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think Then Embed: Generative Context Improves Multimodal Embedding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T00:57:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T23:44:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:13:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-19T16:13:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There is a growing interest in Universal Multimodal Embeddings (UME), where models are required to generate task-specific representations. While recent studies show that Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) perform well on such tasks, they treat MLLMs solely as encoders, overlooking their generative capacity. However, such an encoding paradigm becomes less effective as instructions become more complex and require compositional reasoning. Inspired by the proven effectiveness of chain-of-thought reasoning, we propose a general Think-Then-Embed (TTE) framework for UME, composed of a reasoner and an embedder. The reasoner MLLM first generates reasoning traces that explain complex queries, followed by an embedder that produces representations conditioned on both the original query and the intermediate reasoning. This explicit reasoning step enables more nuanced understanding of complex multimodal instructions. Our contributions are threefold. First, by leveraging a powerful MLLM reasoner, we achieve state-of-the-art performance on the MMEB-V2 benchmark, surpassing proprietary models trained on massive in-house datasets. Second, to reduce the dependency on large MLLM reasoners, we finetune a smaller MLLM reasoner using high-quality embedding-centric reasoning traces, achieving the best performance among open-source models with a 7% absolute gain over recently proposed models. Third, we investigate strategies for integrating the reasoner and embedder into a unified model for improved efficiency without sacrificing performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nevan","familyName":"Wichers","name":"Wichers, Nevan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aram","familyName":"Ebtekar","name":"Ebtekar, Aram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariana","familyName":"Azarbal","name":"Azarbal, Ariana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Gillioz","name":"Gillioz, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christine","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Christine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emil","familyName":"Ryd","name":"Ryd, Emil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neil","familyName":"Rathi","name":"Rathi, Neil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Sleight","name":"Sleight, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Mallen","name":"Mallen, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabien","familyName":"Roger","name":"Roger, Fabien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Marks","name":"Marks, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inoculation Prompting: Instructing LLMs to misbehave at train-time improves test-time alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T03:13:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T21:05:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:11:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are sometimes trained with imperfect oversight signals, leading to undesired behaviors such as reward hacking and sycophancy. Improving oversight quality can be expensive or infeasible, motivating methods that improve learned behavior despite an imperfect training signal. We introduce Inoculation Prompting (IP), a simple but counterintuitive technique that prevents learning of an undesired behavior by modifying training prompts to explicitly request it. For example, to inoculate against reward hacking, we modify the prompts used in supervised fine-tuning to request code that only works on provided test cases but fails on other inputs. Across four settings we find that IP reduces the learning of undesired behavior without substantially reducing the learning of desired capabilities. We also show that prompts which more strongly elicit the undesired behavior prior to fine-tuning more effectively inoculate against the behavior when used during training; this serves as a heuristic to identify promising inoculation prompts. Overall, IP is a simple yet effective way to control how models generalize from fine-tuning, preventing learning of undesired behaviors without substantially disrupting desired capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v2 Updates references. v3 Updates references; Adds IFEval results; Improves appendix readability; Adds author contributions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Nair","name":"Nair, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarik","familyName":"Borogovac","name":"Borogovac, Tarik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Where Did It All Go Wrong? A Hierarchical Look into Multi-Agent Error Attribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:07:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T18:25:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:03:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Error attribution in Large Language Model (LLM) multi-agent systems presents a significant challenge in debugging and improving collaborative AI systems. Current approaches to pinpointing agent and step level failures in interaction traces - whether using all-at-once evaluation, step-by-step analysis, or binary search - fall short when analyzing complex patterns, struggling with both accuracy and consistency. We present ECHO (Error attribution through Contextual Hierarchy and Objective consensus analysis), a novel algorithm that combines hierarchical context representation, objective analysis-based evaluation, and consensus voting to improve error attribution accuracy. Our approach leverages a positional-based leveling of contextual understanding while maintaining objective evaluation criteria, ultimately reaching conclusions through a consensus mechanism. Experimental results demonstrate that ECHO outperforms existing methods across various multi-agent interaction scenarios, showing particular strength in cases involving subtle reasoning errors and complex interdependencies. Our findings suggest that leveraging these concepts of structured, hierarchical context representation combined with consensus-based objective decision-making, provides a more robust framework for error attribution in multi-agent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:02:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peigui","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peigui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunsheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Kunsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafeGuider: Robust and Practical Content Safety Control for Text-to-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:24:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T04:00:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:53:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3719027.3744835","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image models have shown remarkable capabilities in generating high-quality images from natural language descriptions. However, these models are highly vulnerable to adversarial prompts, which can bypass safety measures and produce harmful content. Despite various defensive strategies, achieving robustness against attacks while maintaining practical utility in real-world applications remains a significant challenge. To address this issue, we first conduct an empirical study of the text encoder in the Stable Diffusion (SD) model, which is a widely used and representative text-to-image model. Our findings reveal that the [EOS] token acts as a semantic aggregator, exhibiting distinct distributional patterns between benign and adversarial prompts in its embedding space. Building on this insight, we introduce SafeGuider, a two-step framework designed for robust safety control without compromising generation quality. SafeGuider combines an embedding-level recognition model with a safety-aware feature erasure beam search algorithm. This integration enables the framework to maintain high-quality image generation for benign prompts while ensuring robust defense against both in-domain and out-of-domain attacks. SafeGuider demonstrates exceptional effectiveness in minimizing attack success rates, achieving a maximum rate of only 5.48\\% across various attack scenarios. Moreover, instead of refusing to generate or producing black images for unsafe prompts, SafeGuider generates safe and meaningful images, enhancing its practical utility. In addition, SafeGuider is not limited to the SD model and can be effectively applied to other text-to-image models, such as the Flux model, demonstrating its versatility and adaptability across different architectures. We hope that SafeGuider can shed some light on the practical deployment of secure text-to-image systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM CCS 2025, Code is available at [this https URL](https://github.com/pgqihere/safeguider)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Kondo","name":"Kondo, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. P.","familyName":"How","name":"How, J. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Navigation in Unknown Indoor Environments with Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:26:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T16:08:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel high-level planning framework that leverages vision-language models (VLMs) to improve autonomous navigation in unknown indoor environments with many dead ends. Traditional exploration methods often take inefficient routes due to limited global reasoning and reliance on local heuristics. In contrast, our approach enables a VLM to reason directly about occupancy maps in a zero-shot manner, selecting subgoals that are likely to yield more efficient paths. At each planning step, we convert a 3D occupancy grid into a partial 2D map of the environment, and generate candidate subgoals. Each subgoal is then evaluated and ranked against other candidates by the model. We integrate this planning scheme into DYNUS \\cite{kondo2025dynus}, a state-of-the-art trajectory planner, and demonstrate improved navigation efficiency in simulation. The VLM infers structural patterns (e.g., rooms, corridors) from incomplete maps and balances the need to make progress toward a goal against the risk of entering unknown space. This reduces common greedy failures (e.g., detouring into small rooms) and achieves about 10\\% shorter paths on average."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted to the OWN workshop at IROS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04550","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04550","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenwei","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zhenwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juanhui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juanhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayuan","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jiayuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Mukherjee","name":"Mukherjee, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Suhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiliang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Dumoulin","name":"Dumoulin, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRAJECT-Bench:A Trajectory-Aware Benchmark for Evaluating Agentic Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:30:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T09:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:31:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM)-based agents increasingly rely on tool use to complete real-world tasks. While existing works evaluate the LLMs' tool use capability, they largely focus on the final answers yet overlook the detailed tool usage trajectory, i.e., whether tools are selected, parameterized, and ordered correctly. We introduce TRAJECT-Bench, a trajectory-aware benchmark to comprehensively evaluate LLMs' tool use capability through diverse tasks with fine-grained evaluation metrics. TRAJECT-Bench pairs high-fidelity, executable tools across practical domains with tasks grounded in production-style APIs, and synthesizes trajectories that vary in breadth (parallel calls) and depth (interdependent chains). Besides final accuracy, TRAJECT-Bench also reports trajectory-level diagnostics, including tool selection and argument correctness, and dependency/order satisfaction. Analyses reveal failure modes such as similar tool confusion and parameter-blind selection, and scaling behavior with tool diversity and trajectory length where the bottleneck of transiting from short to mid-length trajectories is revealed, offering actionable guidance for LLMs' tool use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04550","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huikang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongdong","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Dongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptPipe: Memory- and Scheduling-Optimized Pipeline Parallelism for LLM Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T01:06:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pipeline parallelism (PP) has become a standard technique for scaling large language model (LLM) training across multiple devices. However, despite recent progress in reducing memory consumption through activation offloading, existing approaches remain largely heuristic and coarse-grained, often overlooking the fine-grained trade-offs between memory, computation, and scheduling latency. In this work, we revisit the pipeline scheduling problem from a principled optimization perspective. We observe that prevailing strategies either rely on static rules or aggressively offload activations without fully leveraging the interaction between memory constraints and scheduling efficiency. To address this, we formulate scheduling as a constrained optimization problem that jointly accounts for memory capacity, activation reuse, and pipeline bubble minimization. Solving this model yields fine-grained schedules that reduce pipeline bubbles while adhering to strict memory budgets. Our approach complements existing offloading techniques: whereas prior approaches trade memory for time in a fixed pattern, we dynamically optimize the tradeoff with respect to model structure and hardware configuration. Experimental results demonstrate that our method consistently improves both throughput and memory utilization. In particular, we reduce idle pipeline time by up to 50% under the same per-device memory limit, and in some cases, enable the training of larger models within limited memory budgets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Use Mathematical Programming to model Pipeline Parallelism with Offloading to balance efficiency and memory requirement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Jahns","name":"Jahns, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davi","familyName":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Davi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milan","familyName":"Stojkov","name":"Stojkov, Milan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michel A.","familyName":"Kinsy","name":"Kinsy, Michel A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discretized Quadratic Integrate-and-Fire Neuron Model for Deep Spiking Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:30:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) have emerged as energy-efficient alternatives to traditional artificial neural networks, leveraging asynchronous and biologically inspired neuron dynamics. Among existing neuron models, the Leaky Integrate-and-Fire (LIF) neuron has become widely adopted in deep SNNs due to its simplicity and computational efficiency. However, this efficiency comes at the expense of expressiveness, as LIF dynamics are constrained to linear decay at each timestep. In contrast, more complex models, such as the Quadratic Integrate-and-Fire (QIF) neuron, exhibit richer, nonlinear dynamics but have seen limited adoption due to their training instability. On that note, we propose the first discretization of the QIF neuron model tailored for high-performance deep spiking neural networks and provide an in-depth analysis of its dynamics. To ensure training stability, we derive an analytical formulation for surrogate gradient windows directly from our discretizations' parameter set, minimizing gradient mismatch. We evaluate our method on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, ImageNet, and CIFAR-10 DVS, demonstrating its ability to outperform state-of-the-art LIF-based methods. These results establish our discretization of the QIF neuron as a compelling alternative to LIF neurons for deep SNNs, combining richer dynamics with practical scalability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:39Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanzhe","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yuanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yide","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zisu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zisu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruicheng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ruicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xiaoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SATER: A Self-Aware and Token-Efficient Approach to Routing and Cascading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:55:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable performance across diverse tasks, yet their effectiveness frequently depends on costly commercial APIs or cloud services. Model selection thus entails a critical trade-off between performance and cost: high-performing LLMs typically incur substantial expenses, whereas budget-friendly small language models (SLMs) are constrained by limited capabilities. Current research primarily proposes two routing strategies: pre-generation routing and cascade routing. Both approaches have distinct characteristics, with cascade routing typically offering superior cost-effectiveness and accuracy despite its higher latency. To further address the limitations of both approaches, we introduce SATER, a dual-mode compatible approach that fine-tunes models through shortest-response preference optimization and a confidence-aware rejection mechanism. SATER significantly reduces redundant outputs and response times, while improving both the performance of pre-generation routing and the efficiency of cascade routing. Experiments across three SLMs and six datasets, varying in type and complexity, demonstrate that SATER achieves comparable performance while consistently reducing computational costs by over 50\\% and cascade latency by over 80\\%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janos","familyName":"Perczel","name":"Perczel, Janos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Chow","name":"Chow, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dorottya","familyName":"Demszky","name":"Demszky, Dorottya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TeachLM: Post-Training LLMs for Education Using Authentic Learning Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:55:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The promise of generative AI to revolutionize education is constrained by the pedagogical limits of large language models (LLMs). A major issue is the lack of access to high-quality training data that reflect the learning of actual students. Prompt engineering has emerged as a stopgap, but the ability of prompts to encode complex pedagogical strategies in rule-based natural language is inherently limited. To address this gap we introduce TeachLM - an LLM optimized for teaching through parameter-efficient fine-tuning of state-of-the-art models. TeachLM is trained on a dataset comprised of 100,000 hours of one-on-one, longitudinal student-tutor interactions maintained by Polygence, which underwent a rigorous anonymization process to protect privacy. We use parameter-efficient fine-tuning to develop an authentic student model that enables the generation of high-fidelity synthetic student-tutor dialogues. Building on this capability, we propose a novel multi-turn evaluation protocol that leverages synthetic dialogue generation to provide fast, scalable, and reproducible assessments of the dialogical capabilities of LLMs. Our evaluations demonstrate that fine-tuning on authentic learning data significantly improves conversational and pedagogical performance - doubling student talk time, improving questioning style, increasing dialogue turns by 50%, and greater personalization of instruction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Meel","name":"Meel, Shreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sennur","familyName":"Ulukus","name":"Ulukus, Sennur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Distributed Optimization With Feasible Set Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the problem of decentralized constrained optimization with multiple agents $E_1,\\ldots,E_N$ who jointly wish to learn the optimal solution set while keeping their feasible sets $\\mathcal{P}_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal{P}_N$ private from each other. We assume that the objective function $f$ is known to all agents and each feasible set is a collection of points from a universal alphabet $\\mathcal{P}_{alph}$. A designated agent (leader) starts the communication with the remaining (non-leader) agents, and is the first to retrieve the solution set. The leader searches for the solution by sending queries to and receiving answers from the non-leaders, such that the information on the individual feasible sets revealed to the leader should be no more than nominal, i.e., what is revealed from learning the solution set alone. We develop achievable schemes for obtaining the solution set at nominal information leakage, and characterize their communication costs under two communication setups between agents. In this work, we focus on two kinds of network setups: i) ring, where each agent communicates with two adjacent agents, and ii) star, where only the leader communicates with the remaining agents. We show that, if the leader first learns the joint feasible set through an existing private set intersection (PSI) protocol and then deduces the solution set, the information leaked to the leader is greater than nominal. Moreover, we draw connection of our schemes to threshold PSI (ThPSI), which is a PSI-variant where the intersection is revealed only when its cardinality is larger than a threshold value. Finally, for various realizations of $f$ mapped uniformly at random to a fixed range of values, our schemes are more communication-efficient with a high probability compared to retrieving the entire feasible set through PSI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junlin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"givenName":"Jue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jue","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Xu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Athiwaratkun","name":"Athiwaratkun, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhuwan","familyName":"Dhingra","name":"Dhingra, Bhuwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ce","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Staircase Streaming for Low-Latency Multi-Agent Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) opened up new directions for leveraging the collective expertise of multiple LLMs. These methods, such as Mixture-of-Agents, typically employ additional inference steps to generate intermediate outputs, which are then used to produce the final response. While multi-agent inference can enhance response quality, it can significantly increase the time to first token (TTFT), posing a challenge for latency-sensitive applications and hurting user experience. To address this issue, we propose staircase streaming for low-latency multi-agent inference. Instead of waiting for the complete intermediate outputs from previous steps, we begin generating the final response as soon as we receive partial outputs from these steps. Experimental results demonstrate that staircase streaming reduces TTFT by up to 93% while maintaining response quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhou","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Fangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunkit","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Chunkit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yauwai","familyName":"Yim","name":"Yim, Yauwai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Hanabi: Evaluating Multi-Agent Gameplays with Theory-of-Mind and Rationale Inference in Imperfect Information Collaboration Game"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective multi-agent collaboration requires agents to infer the rationale behind others' actions, a capability rooted in Theory-of-Mind (ToM). While recent Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at logical inference, their ability to infer rationale in dynamic, collaborative settings remains under-explored. This study introduces LLM-Hanabi, a novel benchmark that uses the cooperative game Hanabi to evaluate the rationale inference and ToM of LLMs. Our framework features an automated evaluation system that measures both game performance and ToM proficiency. Across a range of models, we find a significant positive correlation between ToM and in-game success. Notably, first-order ToM (interpreting others' intent) correlates more strongly with performance than second-order ToM (predicting others' interpretations). These findings highlight that for effective AI collaboration, the ability to accurately interpret a partner's rationale is more critical than higher-order reasoning. We conclude that prioritizing first-order ToM is a promising direction for enhancing the collaborative capabilities of future models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Wordplay"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:01:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davood","familyName":"Rafiei","name":"Rafiei, Davood","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morgan Lindsay","familyName":"Heisler","name":"Heisler, Morgan Lindsay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Pourreza","name":"Pourreza, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do LLMs Align with My Task? Evaluating Text-to-SQL via Dataset Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:33:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:41:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is an effective method for adapting Large Language Models (LLMs) on downstream tasks. However, variability in training data can hinder a model's ability to generalize across domains. This paper studies the problem of dataset alignment for Natural Language to SQL (NL2SQL or text to SQL), examining how well SFT training data matches the structural characteristics of target queries and how this alignment impacts model performance. We hypothesize that alignment can be accurately estimated by comparing the distributions of structural SQL features across the training set, target data, and the model's predictions prior to SFT. Through comprehensive experiments on three large cross-domain NL2SQL benchmarks and multiple model families, we show that structural alignment is a strong predictor of fine-tuning success. When alignment is high, SFT yields substantial gains in accuracy and SQL generation quality; when alignment is low, improvements are marginal or absent. These findings highlight the importance of alignment-aware data selection for effective fine-tuning and generalization in NL2SQL tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:58:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04898","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04898","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04898","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Jackson","name":"Jackson, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Foerster","name":"Foerster, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimon","familyName":"Whiteson","name":"Whiteson, Shimon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HyperVLA: Efficient Inference in Vision-Language-Action Models via Hypernetworks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:15:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Built upon language and vision foundation models with strong generalization ability and trained on large-scale robotic data, Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have recently emerged as a promising approach to learning generalist robotic policies. However, a key drawback of existing VLAs is their extremely high inference costs. In this paper, we propose HyperVLA to address this problem. Unlike existing monolithic VLAs that activate the whole model during both training and inference, HyperVLA uses a novel hypernetwork (HN)-based architecture that activates only a small task-specific policy during inference, while still retaining the high model capacity needed to accommodate diverse multi-task behaviors during training. Successfully training an HN-based VLA is nontrivial so HyperVLA contains several key algorithm design features that improve its performance, including properly utilizing the prior knowledge from existing vision foundation models, HN normalization, and an action generation strategy. Compared to monolithic VLAs, HyperVLA achieves a similar or even higher success rate for both zero-shot generalization and few-shot adaptation, while significantly reducing inference costs. Compared to OpenVLA, a state-of-the-art VLA model, HyperVLA reduces the number of activated parameters at test time by $90\\times$, and accelerates inference speed by $120\\times$. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/MasterXiong/HyperVLA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04898","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:57:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexia","familyName":"Jolicoeur-Martineau","name":"Jolicoeur-Martineau, Alexia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Less is More: Recursive Reasoning with Tiny Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:58:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:38:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hierarchical Reasoning Model (HRM) is a novel approach using two small neural networks recursing at different frequencies. This biologically inspired method beats Large Language models (LLMs) on hard puzzle tasks such as Sudoku, Maze, and ARC-AGI while trained with small models (27M parameters) on small data (around 1000 examples). HRM holds great promise for solving hard problems with small networks, but it is not yet well understood and may be suboptimal. We propose Tiny Recursive Model (TRM), a much simpler recursive reasoning approach that achieves significantly higher generalization than HRM, while using a single tiny network with only 2 layers. With only 7M parameters, TRM obtains 45% test-accuracy on ARC-AGI-1 and 8% on ARC-AGI-2, higher than most LLMs (e.g., Deepseek R1, o3-mini, Gemini 2.5 Pro) with less than 0.01% of the parameters."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":4,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:56:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongge","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Dongge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camille","familyName":"Couturier","name":"Couturier, Camille","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel Madrigal","familyName":"Diaz","name":"Diaz, Daniel Madrigal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuchao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Rühle","name":"Rühle, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saravan","familyName":"Rajmohan","name":"Rajmohan, Saravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LEGOMem: Modular Procedural Memory for Multi-agent LLM Systems for Workflow Automation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce LEGOMem, a modular procedural memory framework for multi-agent large language model (LLM) systems in workflow automation. LEGOMem decomposes past task trajectories into reusable memory units and flexibly allocates them across orchestrators and task agents to support planning and execution. To explore the design space of memory in multi-agent systems, we use LEGOMem as a lens and conduct a systematic study of procedural memory in multi-agent systems, examining where memory should be placed, how it should be retrieved, and which agents benefit most. Experiments on the OfficeBench benchmark show that orchestrator memory is critical for effective task decomposition and delegation, while fine-grained agent memory improves execution accuracy. We find that even teams composed of smaller language models can benefit substantially from procedural memory, narrowing the performance gap with stronger agents by leveraging prior execution traces for more accurate planning and tool use. These results position LEGOMem as both a practical framework for memory-augmented agent systems and a research tool for understanding memory design in multi-agent workflow automation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:56:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04819","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04819","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04819","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benlin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Benlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amita","familyName":"Kamath","name":"Kamath, Amita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madeleine","familyName":"Grunde-McLaughlin","name":"Grunde-McLaughlin, Madeleine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Winson","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Winson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjay","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Ranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Representations inside the Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:01:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite interpretability work analyzing VIT encoders and transformer activations, we don't yet understand why Multimodal Language Models (MLMs) struggle on perception-heavy tasks. We offer an under-studied perspective by examining how popular MLMs (LLaVA-OneVision, Qwen2.5-VL, and Llama-3-LLaVA-NeXT) process their visual key-value tokens. We first study the flow of visual information through the language model, finding that image value tokens encode sufficient information to perform several perception-heavy tasks zero-shot: segmentation, semantic correspondence, temporal correspondence, and referring expression detection. We find that while the language model does augment the visual information received from the projection of input visual encodings-which we reveal correlates with overall MLM perception capability-it contains less visual information on several tasks than the equivalent visual encoder (SigLIP) that has not undergone MLM finetuning. Further, we find that the visual information corresponding to input-agnostic image key tokens in later layers of language models contains artifacts which reduce perception capability of the overall MLM. Next, we discuss controlling visual information in the language model, showing that adding a text prefix to the image input improves perception capabilities of visual representations. Finally, we reveal that if language models were able to better control their visual information, their perception would significantly improve; e.g., in 33.3% of Art Style questions in the BLINK benchmark, perception information present in the language model is not surfaced to the output! Our findings reveal insights into the role of key-value tokens in multimodal systems, paving the way for deeper mechanistic interpretability of MLMs and suggesting new directions for training their visual encoder and language model components."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04819","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:54:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo Martínez","familyName":"Rivera","name":"Rivera, Eduardo Martínez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Menolascina","name":"Menolascina, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ModernBERT + ColBERT: Enhancing biomedical RAG through an advanced re-ranking retriever"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T12:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a powerful technique for enriching Large Language Models (LLMs) with external knowledge, allowing for factually grounded responses, a critical requirement in high-stakes domains such as healthcare. However, the efficacy of RAG systems is fundamentally restricted by the performance of their retrieval module, since irrelevant or semantically misaligned documents directly compromise the accuracy of the final generated response. General-purpose dense retrievers can struggle with the nuanced language of specialised domains, while the high accuracy of in-domain models is often achieved at prohibitive computational costs. In this work, we aim to address this trade-off by developing and evaluating a two-stage retrieval architecture that combines a lightweight ModernBERT bidirectional encoder for efficient initial candidate retrieval with a ColBERTv2 late-interaction model for fine-grained re-ranking. We conduct comprehensive evaluations of our retriever module performance and RAG system performance in the biomedical context, fine-tuning the IR module using 10k question-passage pairs from PubMedQA. Our analysis of the retriever module confirmed the positive impact of the ColBERT re-ranker, which improved Recall@3 by up to 4.2 percentage points compared to its retrieve-only counterpart. When integrated into the biomedical RAG, our IR module leads to a state-of-the-art average accuracy of 0.4448 on the five tasks of the MIRAGE question-answering benchmark, outperforming strong baselines such as MedCPT (0.4436). Our ablation studies reveal that this performance is critically dependent on a joint fine-tuning process that aligns the retriever and re-ranker; otherwise, the re-ranker might degrade the performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:22:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:22:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luo","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Luo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Siyang","name":"Siyang, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Siyuan","name":"Siyuan, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Zongyuan","name":"Zongyuan, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReactDiff: Fundamental Multiple Appropriate Facial Reaction Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:28:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The automatic generation of diverse and human-like facial reactions in dyadic dialogue remains a critical challenge for human-computer interaction systems. Existing methods fail to model the stochasticity and dynamics inherent in real human reactions. To address this, we propose ReactDiff, a novel temporal diffusion framework for generating diverse facial reactions that are appropriate for responding to any given dialogue context. Our key insight is that plausible human reactions demonstrate smoothness, and coherence over time, and conform to constraints imposed by human facial anatomy. To achieve this, ReactDiff incorporates two vital priors (spatio-temporal facial kinematics) into the diffusion process: i) temporal facial behavioral kinematics and ii) facial action unit dependencies. These two constraints guide the model toward realistic human reaction manifolds, avoiding visually unrealistic jitters, unstable transitions, unnatural expressions, and other artifacts. Extensive experiments on the REACT2024 dataset demonstrate that our approach not only achieves state-of-the-art reaction quality but also excels in diversity and reaction appropriateness."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACM Multimedia"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:21:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:21:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:21:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiding","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhepei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Outcome Reward: Decoupling Search and Answering Improves LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T11:09:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Enabling large language models (LLMs) to utilize search tools offers a promising path to overcoming fundamental limitations such as knowledge cutoffs and hallucinations. Recent work has explored reinforcement learning (RL) for training search-augmented agents that interleave reasoning and retrieval before answering. These approaches usually rely on outcome-based rewards (e.g., exact match), implicitly assuming that optimizing for final answers will also yield effective intermediate search behaviors. Our analysis challenges this assumption: we uncover multiple systematic deficiencies in search that arise under outcome-only training and ultimately degrade final answer quality, including failure to invoke tools, invalid queries, and redundant searches. To address these shortcomings, we introduce DeSA (Decoupling Search-and-Answering), a simple two-stage training framework that explicitly separates search optimization from answer generation. In Stage 1, agents are trained to improve search effectiveness with retrieval recall-based rewards. In Stage 2, outcome rewards are employed to optimize final answer generation. Across seven QA benchmarks, DeSA-trained agents consistently improve search behaviors, delivering substantially higher search recall and answer accuracy than outcome-only baselines. Notably, DeSA outperforms single-stage training approaches that simultaneously optimize recall and outcome rewards, underscoring the necessity of explicitly decoupling the two objectives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanfeng","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Zhanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Bing","name":"Bing, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:44:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly rely on multi-turn tool-integrated planning for knowledge-intensive and complex reasoning tasks. Existing implementations typically rely on a single agent, but they suffer from limited context length and noisy tool responses. A natural solution is to adopt a multi-agent framework with planner- and worker-agents to manage context. However, no existing methods support effective reinforcement learning post-training of tool-integrated multi-agent frameworks. To address this gap, we propose Multi-Agent Tool-Integrated Policy Optimization (MATPO), which enables distinct roles (planner and worker) to be trained within a single LLM instance using role-specific prompts via reinforcement learning. MATPO is derived from a principled credit assignment mechanism across planner and worker rollouts. This design eliminates the need to deploy multiple LLMs, which would be memory-intensive, while preserving the benefits of specialization. Experiments on GAIA-text, WebWalkerQA, and FRAMES show that MATPO consistently outperforms single-agent baselines by an average of 18.38% relative improvement in performance and exhibits greater robustness to noisy tool outputs. Our findings highlight the effectiveness of unifying multiple agent roles within a single LLM and provide practical insights for stable and efficient multi-agent RL training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04668","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04668","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04668","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Habin","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Habin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeongseob","familyName":"Won","name":"Won, Yeongseob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juwon","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Juwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeong-Moon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Gyeong-Moon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ConceptSplit: Decoupled Multi-Concept Personalization of Diffusion Models via Token-wise Adaptation and Attention Disentanglement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T10:22:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, multi-concept personalization for text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models to represent several subjects in an image has gained much more attention. The main challenge of this task is \"concept mixing\", where multiple learned concepts interfere or blend undesirably in the output image. To address this issue, in this paper, we present ConceptSplit, a novel framework to split the individual concepts through training and inference. Our framework comprises two key components. First, we introduce Token-wise Value Adaptation (ToVA), a merging-free training method that focuses exclusively on adapting the value projection in cross-attention. Based on our empirical analysis, we found that modifying the key projection, a common approach in existing methods, can disrupt the attention mechanism and lead to concept mixing. Second, we propose Latent Optimization for Disentangled Attention (LODA), which alleviates attention entanglement during inference by optimizing the input latent. Through extensive qualitative and quantitative experiments, we demonstrate that ConceptSplit achieves robust multi-concept personalization, mitigating unintended concept interference. Code is available at https://github.com/KU-VGI/ConceptSplit"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 13 figures, to be published in ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04668","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:20:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:20:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:20:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo","familyName":"Kneissl","name":"Kneissl, Carlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Bülte","name":"Bülte, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Scholl","name":"Scholl, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gitta","familyName":"Kutyniok","name":"Kutyniok, Gitta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improved probabilistic regression using diffusion models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Probabilistic regression models the entire predictive distribution of a response variable, offering richer insights than classical point estimates and directly allowing for uncertainty quantification. While diffusion-based generative models have shown remarkable success in generating complex, high-dimensional data, their usage in general regression tasks often lacks uncertainty-related evaluation and remains limited to domain-specific applications. We propose a novel diffusion-based framework for probabilistic regression that learns predictive distributions in a nonparametric way. More specifically, we propose to model the full distribution of the diffusion noise, enabling adaptation to diverse tasks and enhanced uncertainty quantification. We investigate different noise parameterizations, analyze their trade-offs, and evaluate our framework across a broad range of regression tasks, covering low- and high-dimensional settings. For several experiments, our approach shows superior performance against existing baselines, while delivering calibrated uncertainty estimates, demonstrating its versatility as a tool for probabilistic prediction."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:18:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naman","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Naman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreeyash","familyName":"Gowaikar","name":"Gowaikar, Shreeyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirankumar","familyName":"Shiragur","name":"Shiragur, Kirankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramakrishna B","familyName":"Bairi","name":"Bairi, Ramakrishna B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishikesh","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, Rishikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritabrata","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Ritabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankarshan","familyName":"Damle","name":"Damle, Sankarshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shachee Mishra","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shachee Mishra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COSMIR: Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning over Long Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning over very long inputs remains difficult for large language models (LLMs). Common workarounds either shrink the input via retrieval (risking missed evidence), enlarge the context window (straining selectivity), or stage multiple agents to read in pieces. In staged pipelines (e.g., Chain of Agents, CoA), free-form summaries passed between agents can discard crucial details and amplify early mistakes. We introduce COSMIR (Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning), a chain-style framework that replaces ad hoc messages with a structured memory. A Planner agent first turns a user query into concrete, checkable sub-questions. worker agents process chunks via a fixed micro-cycle: Extract, Infer, Refine, writing all updates to the shared memory. A Manager agent then Synthesizes the final answer directly from the memory. This preserves step-wise read-then-reason benefits while changing both the communication medium (structured memory) and the worker procedure (fixed micro-cycle), yielding higher faithfulness, better long-range aggregation, and auditability. On long-context QA from the HELMET suite, COSMIR reduces propagation-stage information loss and improves accuracy over a CoA baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Honghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Wei","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kai-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextNav: Towards Agentic Multimodal In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances demonstrate that multimodal large language models (MLLMs) exhibit strong multimodal in-context learning (ICL) capabilities, enabling them to adapt to novel vision-language tasks from a few contextual examples. However, existing ICL approaches face challenges in reconciling scalability with robustness across diverse tasks and noisy contextual examples: manually selecting examples produces clean contexts but is labor-intensive and task-specific, while similarity-based retrieval improves scalability but could introduce irrelevant or structurally inconsistent samples that degrade ICL performance. To address these limitations, we propose ContextNav, the first agentic framework that integrates the scalability of automated retrieval with the quality and adaptiveness of human-like curation, enabling noise-robust and dynamically optimized contextualization for multimodal ICL. ContextNav unifies context management and noise-robust contextualization within a closed-loop workflow driven by graph-based orchestration. Specifically, it builds a resource-aware multimodal embedding pipeline, maintains a retrievable vector database, and applies agentic retrieval and structural alignment to construct noise-resilient contexts. An Operational Grammar Graph (OGG) further supports adaptive workflow planning and optimization, enabling the agent to refine its operational strategies based on downstream ICL feedback. Experimental results demonstrate that ContextNav achieves state-of-the-art performance across various datasets, underscoring the promise of agentic workflows for advancing scalable and robust contextualization in multimodal ICL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianrui","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRACE: Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prevailing methods for training Large Language Models (LLMs) as text encoders rely on contrastive losses that treat the model as a black box function, discarding its generative and reasoning capabilities in favor of static embeddings. We introduce GRACE (Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization), a novel framework that reimagines contrastive signals not as losses to be minimized, but as rewards that guide a generative policy. In GRACE, the LLM acts as a policy that produces explicit, human-interpretable rationales--structured natural language explanations of its semantic understanding. These rationales are then encoded into high-quality embeddings via mean pooling. Using policy gradient optimization, we train the model with a multi-component reward function that maximizes similarity between query positive pairs and minimizes similarity with negatives. This transforms the LLM from an opaque encoder into an interpretable agent whose reasoning process is transparent and inspectable. On MTEB benchmark, GRACE yields broad cross category gains: averaged over four backbones, the supervised setting improves overall score by 11.5% over base models, and the unsupervised variant adds 6.9%, while preserving general capabilities. This work treats contrastive objectives as rewards over rationales, unifying representation learning with generation to produce stronger embeddings and transparent rationales. The model, data and code are available at https://github.com/GasolSun36/GRACE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 7 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":50,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04506+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04514+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04520+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04532+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04547+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04550+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04560+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04568+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04573+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04583+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04584+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04607+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04618+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04641+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04668+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04678+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04695+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04712+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04757+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04767+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04773+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04800+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04819+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04850+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04851+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04852+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04860+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04871+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04886+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04898+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04919+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04980+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.04991+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05014+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05024+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05038+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05059+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05068+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05069+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05077+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05087+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05097+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05164+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05168+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05173+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05174+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05176+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05186+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05188+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.05192%29"}}